<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=longbow">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Weapons and war</p><h1 class="enc-title">Longbow</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3300 BCE</span> · Also known as English longbow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A longbow is a tall bow that makes a long draw possible, usually a self bow cut from a single piece of wood. The bow found in 1991 with the mummy known as Ötzi in the Ötztal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> was made of yew and 1.82 metres long, and the body has been dated to around 3300 BCE. Since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> European longbows were made mainly from yew, or from wych elm where yew was scarce. English and Welsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> used them in large numbers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> in 1346, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poitiers">Poitiers</a> in 1356 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> in 1415, and the weapon stayed in use in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> long after effective firearms appeared.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/longbow.jpg" alt="Longbow" width="330" height="85" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hitchhiker89 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Englishlongbow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The coins show Hellenistic uniforms, helmets either round in the Greco-Bactrian style or the flat Macedonian kausia of Apollodotus I's coins, and weapons of spear, sword, <b>longbow</b> and arrows.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3300 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Ötztal Alps</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>A slightly shorter yew bow from Rotten Bottom, Tweedsmuir, Scotland, has a calibrated radiocarbon date of 4040 to 3640 BCE; European longbows are also said to date from the Paleolithic.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/archery">Bow and arrow<span>c. 80,000 years ago</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=longbow">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
